        "original_text": "Let E be a continuous real-valued energy curve with an exact one-sided derivative E'(u)=-qD(u) at every time. Let r>0 and suppose rE(u)<=qD(u)+b for every real u. Then for every s<=t, E(t)<=E(s) exp(-r(t-s))+(b/r)(1-exp(-r(t-s))). This scalar comparison does not construct a semigroup or establish the derivative identity or coercivity estimate for a concrete model.",
        "text_sha256": "5525492917326b5073bbd12f7c1656ae71764d4c7a07f3f9e82bcf3ae57774e3"
      },
      "lean": {
        "declaration": "AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.FunctionalInequalities.SemigroupDecay.forced_exponential_decay_of_scaled_dissipation_from",
        "file": "AutoSamplingTheory/TechnicalLemmas/FunctionalInequalities/ForcedSemigroupDecay.lean",
        "statement": "theorem forced_exponential_decay_of_scaled_dissipation_from\n    {scale rate forcing : ℝ} (hrate : 0 < rate)\n    (curve : DissipationCurve scale)\n    (hcoercive : ∀ u : ℝ,\n      rate * curve.energy u ≤ scale * curve.dissipation u + forcing)\n    {s t : ℝ} (hst : s ≤ t) :\n    curve.energy t ≤\n      curve.energy s * Real.exp (-rate * (t - s)) +\n        (forcing / rate) * (1 - Real.exp (-rate * (t - s)))",
