            "The forced coercivity inequality rE(u)≤qD(u)+b holds for every real u.",
            "The terminal time satisfies s≤t."
          ],
          "steps": [
            {
              "title": "Convert coercivity into a differential inequality",
              "text": "Negating the forced coercivity inequality and using the derivative identity gives E'(u)≤-rE(u)+b.",
              "formula": "E'(u)=-qD(u)\\le-rE(u)+b",
              "lean": "The proof combines `neg_le_neg (hcoercive u)` with linear arithmetic."
            },
            {
              "title": "Apply one-sided Grönwall",
              "text": "Mathlib's right-slope Grönwall theorem applies directly because the curve is continuous and the exact right derivative controls every right slope.",
              "formula": "E(t)\\le\\operatorname{gronwallBound}(E(s),-r,b,t-s)",
              "lean": "Use `le_gronwallBound_of_liminf_deriv_right_le` with K=-r and ε=b."
            },
            {
              "title": "Expose the forcing floor",
              "text": "Since r is nonzero, unfold the closed Grönwall expression and rewrite b/(-r)(e^{-rΔ}-1) as (b/r)(1-e^{-rΔ}).",
              "formula": "\\frac{b}{-r}(e^{-r\\Delta}-1)=\\frac{b}{r}(1-e^{-r\\Delta})",
              "lean": "Unfold `gronwallBound`; `field_simp` and `ring` verify the scalar identity."
            }
